[slope] The Y-seam floor tongue's full-width 45-degree scarf nose — this is the bed-side face of the six-millimetre floor overlap, not a corbel hanging from either X wall. The tongue's broad underside is on the print bed and this face rises one millimetre per millimetre from it, leaving the cold-core bearing surface flat while lapping the front and back floor slabs.
file: hardware/printed-parts/enclosure/enclosure/enclosure-front-bottom.step
click: x=0.000 y=209.838 z=-3.000

[slope] The condenser flank vents' 45-degree closing hips — each is a 1.55-millimetre run-out inside its groove's shadow, made by the void cutter rather than an added wall ledge. At the sill material recedes as the standing print climbs, and at the ceiling each opening closes at the enclosure's supported 45-degree relief angle; the lint pairs these YZ hip faces with a remote X wall even though they do not hang from that wall.
